<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mausoleum at Halicarnassus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">353–351 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tomb of Mausolus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mausoleum at Halicarnassus was a tomb built between 353 and 351 BCE in Halicarnassus, present-day Bodrum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausolus">Mausolus</a>, a Carian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Persian Empire</a>, and his sister-wife Artemisia II. Designed by the Greek architects Satyros of Paros and Pythius of Priene after the tombs of Lycia, it stood about 45 m high, with reliefs on its four sides by Leochares, Bryaxis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scopas">Scopas</a> and Timotheus and 400 freestanding sculptures. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> counted it among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-wonders-of-the-ancient-world">Seven Wonders of the Ancient World</a>. Earthquakes from the 12th to the 15th century destroyed it, and its name gave the word mausoleum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mausoleum-at-halicarnassus.jpg" alt="Mausoleum at Halicarnassus" width="330" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jona Lendering · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mausoleum_at_Halicarnassus_at_the_Bodrum_Museum_of_Underwater_Archaeology.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the 4th century BCE Halicarnassus was the capital of the small kingdom of Caria, within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. Hecatomnus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milas">Milas</a> left it to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausolus">Mausolus</a> in 377 BCE. Mausolus extended his rule along the south-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and invaded Lycia, whose monumental tombs inspired his own. Though of local descent, he spoke Greek and admired Greek government. He and Artemisia embellished Halicarnassus with marble at great cost to the taxpayer. When he died in 353 BCE the project was continued by Artemisia, who survived him by only two years. Modern historians believe construction began before his death, as two years would not have sufficed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The tomb stood on a hill above the city in an enclosed courtyard, on a stone platform reached by a stairway flanked by stone lions. A marble block covered with reliefs of the battle of centaurs and Lapiths and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> fighting Amazons rose for a third of the height of about 45 m. Above it came thirty-six slender columns, with a statue between each pair, and a pyramidal roof surmounted by a quadriga in which rode images of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausolus">Mausolus</a> and Artemisia. According to Pliny, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scopas">Scopas</a> carved the north side, Bryaxis the east, Timotheus the south and Leochares the west. The urns with the ashes of Mausolus and Artemisia were placed in the unfinished tomb.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The monument was untouched when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> took the city in 334 BCE. When it fell is unknown, but when the Knights of St John arrived in 1402 it was in ruins. The knights used its stones to fortify Bodrum Castle and burned much of the marble into lime. In 1852 Charles Thomas Newton was sent to find the tomb. He bought a plot where Pliny's account suggested it lay and dug tunnels under neighbouring plots until he found three corners of the foundation, then recovered reliefs, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> wheel and the statues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mausolus">Mausolus</a> and Artemisia. Today only the foundation and a small museum remain.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mausoleum at Halicarnassus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
